<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Six-Day War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as June War, Third Arab–Israeli War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Six-Day War was the war of 5-10 June 1967 in which Israel, striking first after weeks of crisis, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Jordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and captured the Sinai Peninsula, Gaza, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> with East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>. Fought in less than a week, it tripled the territory under Israeli control, began the occupation that has defined the conflict ever since, and reshaped politics across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In May 1967 the deployment of troops to Sinai, the expulsion of UN peacekeepers and the closure of the Straits of Tiran precipitated the <b>Six-Day War</b>, in which Israel took Sinai and Gaza.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The army lost two-thirds of the Golan Heights to Israel in under 48 hours in the <b>Six-Day War</b> of 1967, retreated from Jordan in Black September 1970, and with Egypt launched the Yom Kippur War on 6 October 1973, in which Israel reversed its gains and destroyed Quneitra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Six-Day War</b> of 1967 religious Zionists came to see settling the West Bank as a commandment of God, and religious Zionist ideology motivated the assassination of Yitzhak Rabin in 1995.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Israel’s defeat of Arab armies in the <b>Six-Day War</b> of 1967 was blamed by some on the secular nationalism of their regimes, and Ba’athism, Arab socialism and Arab nationalism lost credibility while Islamist movements inspired by Maududi and Qutb gained ground.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>To a lesser extent, the defeats by Israel, and above all the <b>Six-Day War</b> of 1967, together with the failure of Egypt and Syria to generate economic growth, damaged the ideology’s credibility.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Three weeks after its first performance the <b>Six-Day War</b> broke out, and the song quickly became a morale song for the Israel Defense Forces; Shemer had sung it for soldiers even before the festival.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jerusalem-of-gold">Jerusalem of Gold</a></li></ul></section>
